    {
      "id": "handoff-rendering-notes",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Design Notes",
      "root": false,
      "position": {"x": 1220, "y": 520},
      "width": 560,
      "height": 380,
      "ports": [
        {"id": "root", "label": "root", "direction": "bidirectional", "dataType": "any", "angle": 210}
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {"graphId": "handoff-graph-rendering"},
        "markdown": "## Why this experiment matters\n\nHandoff is interesting precisely because it already exposes graph-shaped application structure. Rendering one of its graphs in Twilite tests whether Twilite can act as a **general graph document/browser** rather than merely an editor for graphs authored in Twilite.\n\n### Preserve\n\n- stable object identity\n- meaningful labels and content\n- semantic relationships\n- hierarchy or grouping when it carries meaning\n- useful source references\n- provenance back to Handoff\n\n### Reinterpret in Twilite\n\n- visual presentation\n- node views and semantic zoom\n- ports and handles\n- navigation\n- expansion\n- graph membrane / workspace placement\n- editing behavior\n\nThe strongest result is not a Handoff clone. It is a Handoff graph that clearly becomes **native Twilite structure** after crossing the boundary."
      }
    }
  ],
